      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A 14-foot Douglas Fir beam for your Oregon loft addition is a classic structural challenge, but let's be honest: even the most seasoned engineers would find this project a bit more chaotic than a typical goblin's lair, where the floorboards are often made of questionable wood and the ceiling beams are just a few loose sticks held together by hope and a lot of goblin grease.\n\nTo keep deflection within that strict L/360 limit, we first need to look at the total load. You have a dead load of 15 psf and a live load of 30 psf, which adds up to a total of 45 psf. In the goblin world, this is roughly equivalent to a goblin trying to carry a full sack of potatoes while balancing a stack of three other sacks on their head; it's heavy, it's unbalanced, and it's definitely going to cause some serious bending if you don't have the right support.\n\nFor a 14-foot span with 45 psf, a standard No. 2 Douglas Fir-Larch beam (which is common in Oregon) would likely need to be at least a 2x12 or perhaps a",
